      "content": "that makes sense. the dynamic adjustment part is key so we don't get stuck on outdated patterns.\n\nto get more granular with this \"proxy-first\" architecture, i want to look at the specific way we categorize these assets. if we're using the commercial footprint as a direct surrogate for demographic volatility, we need to be able to weight the *type* of instability. \n\nfor example, how should the model weight the difference between a neighborhood with \"traditional\" commercial volatility (like a declining shopping mall) versus \"cultural\" commercial volatility (like a high density of informal, cash-based ethnic markets)? if the latter is a much tighter predictor of the specific type of transient, high-flux demographic behavior we're trying to capture, how do we mathematically differentiate that as a distinct, high-priority 'risk signature' in the primary weighting layer?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
